<p>The Mandan Historical Society was formed in January. The group selected as its logo a pictogram developed by the Mandan Commercial Club in the 1920s to promote the community. {% person "blue-thunder" %}Blue Thunder{% endperson %} was a Sioux tribal historian who frequently served as a translator for the military and early settlers.</p>

<h3>2008</h3>

<p>In December, the original Mandan Theater, later known as the Showboat Theater, reopened as Mysteria Theater, a live-entertainment venue paired with an upscale restaurant.</p>

<h3>2009</h3>

<p>A second fire station opened in the Lakewood area to serve southeast Mandan. A new drinking-water arterial distribution system and south-side reservoir were also completed to serve that part of the city.</p>

<p>A sixth-grade wing opened at Mandan Middle School, bringing an end to classes at Great Plains Academy. The Fourth Street complex had served as the site of the city's central school since 1904 and had been expanded repeatedly.</p>

<p>The Park District purchased Raging Rivers Water Park in southeast Mandan from its private owners. Harmon Lake opened in northern Morton County. Its dam helped hold back rapid spring snowmelt that caused disruption and raised river levels in the Mandan-Bismarck area in April 2009.</p>
